   "title": "Lee's approval rating falls to 35.3% after rebound: poll",
   "url": "https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/southkorea/politics/20261026/lees-approval-rating-falls-to-353-after-rebound-poll",
   "domain": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "source": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "published": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 03:47:04 GMT",
   "_dt": 1792986424.0,
   "ageDays": 0.02,
   "summary": "President Lee Jae Myung's approval rating fell to 35.3 percent after rebounding the previous week, a poll showed Monday. In the poll conducted on 2,517 adults nationwide from last Monday to Friday by Realmeter, positive assessments of Lee's job performance fell 2 percentage points from the previous week. Lee's disapproval rating stood at 62.3 percent, up 1.9 percentage points from the previous week.",
   "summary_zh": "一项周一公布的民意调查显示，李在明总统的支持率在前一周回升后降至35.3%。Realmeter上周一至周五对全国2,517名成年人开展的调查显示，对李施政表现的正面评价较前一周下降2个百分点。李的不支持率为62.3%，较前一周上升1.9个百分点。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "President Lee Jae Myung's approval rating fell to 35.3 percent after rebounding the previous week, a poll showed Monday. In the poll conducted on 2,517 adults nationwide from last Monday to Friday by Realmeter, positive assessments of Lee's job performance fell 2 percentage points from the previous week. Lee's disapproval rating stood at 62.3 percent, up 1.9 percentage points from the previous week. Realmeter attributed the decline to growing concerns over the housing market and the economy, including the recent stock market plunge. The pollster also cited North Korea's threat to resume loudspeaker broadcasts toward the South and controversy over whether the chief of the Serious Crime Investigation Agency should attend Cabinet meetings. In a separate survey of 1,003 adults conducted between Thursday and Friday, support for the ruling Democratic Party of Korea fell 0.2 percentage point to 36 percent, while that of the main opposition People Power Party rose 1.7 percentage points to 42.6 percent. The survey on the president had a margin of error of 2 percentage points at a 95 percent confidence level, while the one on political parties had a margin of error of 3.1 percentage points at a 95 percent confidence level",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
