   "title": "The need for a national media council",
   "url": "https://www.thehindu.com/opinion/op-ed/the-need-for-a-national-media-council/article71624323.ece",
   "domain": "thehindu.com",
   "source": "thehindu.com",
   "published": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 08:36:27 +0530",
   "_dt": 1792983987.0,
   "ageDays": 0.05,
   "summary": "43 journalists detained, several assaulted, while covering protests against CEC: CPJ Journalists, protesters, MP allege police brutality; cops cite non-cooperation during detentions Journalists detained in Delhi’s massive police crackdown on youth protest One solution could be a national media council or commission. Just like you need a law degree to practise law, a medical degree to become a doctor, the media council may mandate a degree or diploma in journalism and mass communication while applying for a licence.",
   "summary_zh": "记者报道反对中央选举委员会（CEC）的抗议活动时，43名记者被拘留，数人遭殴打；CPJ记者、抗议者和议员指控警方使用暴力手段；警察则称拘留行动期间记者不予配合。在德里大规模警力镇压青年抗议行动期间，记者遭到拘留。一种解决方案或许是设立全国媒体委员会或媒体专员机构。正如从事法律职业需要法律学位、成为医生需要医学学位一样，媒体委员会可以规定，申请新闻与大众传播从业许可须持有相关学位或文凭。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription The recent protests called by the Cockroach Janta Party (CJP) , and helmed by Opposition parties, demanding the resignation of Chief Election Commissioner Gyanesh Kumar have exposed a chink in journalists’ armour. The crackdown on journalists, especially freelancers and independent journalists, during the October 10 protests , has rekindled the debate on who has the right to be recognised as a journalist and about who decides who is a journalist. One has seen how during the CJP protests , both in July over the NEET paper leak and in October over the demand for the CEC’s removal, freelancers and independent journalists, and content creators descended in hordes at the protest venue already bulging at the seams with reporters from registered media houses. However, during the second round of protests on October 10, phalanxes of riot police removed them from the protest site and sent them to detention centres. Only journalists with the Press Information Bureau (PIB) or Directorate of Information and Publicity (DIP) accreditation were allowed to cover the protests. A Bench comprising Chief Justice of India (CJI) Surya Kant and Justice Joymalya Bagchi, while hearing a Public Interest Litigation (PIL) which seeks the protection of the right to freedom of expression by freelance journalists and content creators, wondered whether the right to freedom of expression empowered every citizen of this country to be a journalist. “If everyone starts becoming a journalist, it will create problems for every other person,” the CJI said. During the hearing, senior advocate Indira Jaisingh said that there must be a national protocol for police not to obstruct the professional work of any journalist who has an identity card from the employer. Solicitor General Tushar Mehta said that in the present era, any person with a mobile phone could claim to be an independent or freelance journalist when he/she would, in fact, be making reels only to garner popularity on social media to earn money by either presenting one-sided stories or sensationalising events. 43 journalists detained, several assaulted, while covering protests against CEC: CPJ Journalists, protesters, MP allege police brutality; cops cite non-cooperation during detentions Journalists detained in Delhi’s massive police crackdown on youth protest One solution could be a national media council or commission. Just like there is the Bar Council of India for lawyers and an Institute of Chartered Accountants of India for chartered accountants, the media council can recognise all working journalists, freelance journalists, camerapersons and technicians. Just like you need a law degree to practise law, a medical degree to become a doctor, the media council may mandate a degree or diploma in journalism and mass communication while applying for a licence. A few years ago, the Press Club of India drafted a Media Transparency (and Accountability) Bill , which mandated the formation of a national media council, among other things. Recently, some other journalists’ bodies have also submitted a memorandum to the government with the same demand. As more and more people with a camera claim to be freelance journalists, it may be high time for the government to give some serious thought to the idea of such a council. Rakesh Goswami is Professor of Journalism at Indian Institute of Mass Communication, New Delhi news media / Cockroach Janta Party / demonstration / protest / government Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
