   "title": "4 ways US foreign policy could change if Democrats win in November",
   "url": "https://www.rappler.com/world/us-canada/changes-usa-foreign-policy-victory-midterm-elections-2026/",
   "domain": "rappler.com",
   "source": "rappler.com",
   "published": "Sun, 25 Oct 2026 18:20:59 +0800",
   "_dt": 1792923659.0,
   "ageDays": 0.74,
   "summary": "WASHINGTON, USA – Nonpartisan analysts say Democrats stand a good chance of winning the US House of Representatives and possibly the Senate in the November 3 midterm elections , which would give them a slice of power in Washington after two years of Republican control. Here are some ways a Democratic Congress could hem in Trump’s foreign policy. With a victory in both chambers in November, Democrats would be able to decide what gets voted on and when.",
   "summary_zh": "美国华盛顿——无党派分析人士表示，在11月3日的中期选举中，民主党很有可能赢得美国众议院，甚至可能赢得参议院。共和党掌控华盛顿两年后，民主党将由此在华盛顿获得部分权力。以下是民主党控制的国会可能限制特朗普外交政策的几种方式。如果民主党在11月两院选举中获胜，就能决定哪些事项付诸表决以及何时表决。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Already have Rappler+? Sign in to listen to groundbreaking journalism. A man holds a 'Vote Blue' sign while sitting in a golf cart during a Democratic rally in The Villages, Florida, US, October 17, 2026. WASHINGTON, USA – Nonpartisan analysts say Democrats stand a good chance of winning the US House of Representatives and possibly the Senate in the November 3 midterm elections , which would give them a slice of power in Washington after two years of Republican control. Democrats argue that Republicans have allowed President Donald Trump ‘s administration to declare war and control spending, even though the US Constitution reserves those powers for lawmakers. Here are some ways a Democratic Congress could hem in Trump’s foreign policy. Trump did not secure approval from Congress before he launched the war with Iran on February 28 , and Democrats have voted repeatedly to end the fighting unless he does so. One of these resolutions passed with support from a handful of Republicans, but Trump’s administration ignored it. With control of the House or the Senate, Democrats could cut defense spending to pressure Trump to obtain permission from Congress to continue the war. They could also require administration officials to testify publicly about their plans. Trump has largely halted direct US financial and military aid to Ukraine, despite broad support from Democratic and Republican lawmakers as it fights a Russian invasion . Most recently, he has angered members of Congress, including many Republicans, by lifting sanctions on Russian diesel and waiving sanctions on Russian energy only weeks after signing them into law. With a victory in both chambers in November, Democrats would be able to decide what gets voted on and when. That could allow them to approve new aid to Ukraine, which likely would pass with the two-thirds majorities needed to overcome a Trump veto. They could also target other Trump activities. For example, Representative Gregory Meeks of New York, who could head the House Foreign Affairs Committee if Democrats win control, has introduced bipartisan legislation to block Trump’s plan to bring Russian diesel back into the global market. Support for Israel has plummeted among Democrats over its conduct in the Gaza war, which has killed tens of thousands of Palestinian civilians. The ongoing violence in the Israeli-occupied West Bank and Israeli Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu’s close alliance with Republicans have also been factors. Democrats could limit or end military aid to Israel, which amounted to $3.8 billion in the most recent fiscal year. That is more than was provided to any other country. Nearly half of House Democrats voted this summer to cut off aid to Israel. Some of those who voted against that effort, such as Meeks, have backed limits on providing weapons like heavy bombs to Israel. Democrats are far more likely than Trump’s Republicans to limit military spending. Trump asked for an unprecedented 67% increase in the military budget this year, to $1.5 trillion, but Democrats blocked it in the Senate, citing his refusal to consult Congress about the Iran war and his steep cuts to social programs. Trump has also withheld or canceled billions of dollars in foreign aid approved by Congress. Many Democrats have been angered by the cuts and say they will try to restore funding. Upgrade to Rappler+ for exclusive content and unlimited access",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "ru"
   ],
   "topic": [
    "pol",
    "sec"
   ],
   "tier": 2,
   "actor": "美国",
