   "title": ". Is it a coincidence that the areas with rare earth in Myanmar are also conflict zones?",
   "url": "https://x.com/World_Data_A/status/2113573395027624257",
   "snippet": ".\nIs it a coincidence that the areas with rare earth in Myanmar are also conflict zones?\n\nIn northern Myanmar’s Kachin State, near the Chinese border, rare earth deposits overlap with areas controlled by armed groups like the KIO.\n\nAfter the KIO took control of the Chipwi–Pangwa area, China initially closed the border for rare earth-related imports and exports.\n\nRare earth mining in Myanmar has become closely tied to local conflicts. Armed groups use mining revenues to fund their operations, while China seeks stable access to these critical minerals. The result is a pattern where resource-rich border regions are also hotspots of fighting.\nSource: @CrisisGroup by Richard Horsey, Thomas Kean, Carolin Graf and David Muchiri Kimotho\nVisualisations and imagery by Claire Boccon-Gibod, Paul Franz, Ysé Angerer and Jorge Gutiérrez Lucena",
   "body": ".\nIs it a coincidence that the areas with rare earth in Myanmar are also conflict zones?\n\nIn northern Myanmar’s Kachin State, near the Chinese border, rare earth deposits overlap with areas controlled by armed groups like the KIO.\n\nAfter the KIO took control of the Chipwi–Pangwa area, China initially closed the border for rare earth-related imports and exports.\n\nRare earth mining in Myanmar has become closely tied to local conflicts. Armed groups use mining revenues to fund their operations, while China seeks stable access to these critical minerals. The result is a pattern where resource-rich border regions are also hotspots of fighting.\nSource: @CrisisGroup by Richard Horsey, Thomas Kean, Carolin Graf and David Muchiri Kimotho\nVisualisations and imagery by Claire Boccon-Gibod, Paul Franz, Ysé Angerer and Jorge Gutiérrez Lucena",
